In article <CEy5GA.9rM@dscomsa.desy.de> pawlak@zeubac.desy.de writes:
>
>In article <1993Oct14.165006.18191@gtc.com>, dav@gtc.com (David L. Markowitz) writes:
...[for save file portability]
>>Better yet, use XDR (eXternal Data Representation).  This is part of Sun's 
>>ONC/XDR standard.
>
>Don't want to start a flame war but... how many other vendors support 
>that "standard"?....

That standard is the one used to build NFS.  It is about as standard
as anything you could hope for.  It is available on all major unix
platforms (DEC, SUN, IBM, HP, Acorn :), ...) and is also available on
others including VMS and MacOS (and even MessyDos).

>But: you have to ensure not only that the data is readable across 
>platforms, but also that you have essentially the same version of nethack 
>on both sides (identical settings of compilation options inclusive). That
>might be a bit dificult with the high 'configurability' of nethack (well,
>its easy to write the options into save file, read them back and check,
>but it might be difficult to ensure that nethack is really identically
>configured on your different machines). 

An excellent point... the configuration must be checked and the version
information as well.  Ideally, some procedure could be set up to overcome
some variation in configuration (eg, move a game from one without SUCCUBI
to one with... might be harder to do the reverse).  If the compilation
options are stored as well as the version information, then older save
files could be recognized.  Ideally these could be converted to the new
version on the fly (or deleted if that is impossible).

